```python
def classify_four_bar(ground, crank, coupler, rocker):
    links = {"ground": ground, "crank": crank, "coupler": coupler, "rocker": rocker}
    s = min(links.values())
    l = max(links.values())
    if s + l > sum(links.values()) - s - l:
        return "non-Grashof"
    shortest = min(links, key=links.get)
    if shortest == "ground":
        return "double-crank"
    if shortest == "coupler":
        return "double-rocker"
    return "crank-rocker"
```

The Grashof condition decides whether *any* link can turn fully relative to the others; which link is shortest then decides which one it is, as seen from the ground. The lesson's linkage (100, 30, 90, 70) is a crank–rocker; (100, 45, 50, 60) has $s + l = 145 > p + q = 110$, so every link only rocks. Note that the cases with $s + l = p + q$ (change-point linkages) are classified here like Grashof ones; a fuller classifier would flag them, because they can switch branch at the flat position.
